      {/*
        The band takes the film's own 16:9 rather than a share of the window, so the
        whole frame shows. At 78vh it was about 2.3:1 and a cover fit cropped the top
        and bottom off every shot.

        [BUG FIX] A full-bleed 16:9 band is taller than the window on a laptop: 851px at
        1512 and 1080px at 1920, against perhaps 800px of usable viewport. Nothing was
        cropping the film — the figure measured 16:9, the video measured 16:9 inside it
        under `object-fit: contain`, with no transform and no spill, and a frame pulled
        from the shipped encode is pixel-identical to the same frame of the practice's
        source. The band simply could not be seen all at once, which reads as a crop
        while you scroll, and is what the practice reported.

        The band is capped at 86vh so the whole frame fits on screen, and the cap is
        applied to its WIDTH — width:min(100%, 86vh*16/9) — so the band stays exactly
        16:9 and simply centres itself when the window is too short to carry it full
        bleed.

        86vh and not 78vh: the first attempt at this cap left the film reading small —
        1109px wide in a 1512px window, 73% of it. The constraint is the gap between
        the header's bottom edge and the foot of the window, which is about 713px in an
        800px window once the header has shrunk to 87px on scroll. 86vh is 688px there,
        so it still clears the header, and the film comes out 1223px wide — 81% of the
        window, a tenth larger than at 78vh. Measured clearance is positive at every
        window shape checked, not assumed. Capping the height instead would have left the film in a 2.4:1 box with
        200px of dark matte down each side, which looks like a video player rather than
        like the rest of this site; a centred plate with page margins matches the other
